<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
    <RootNamespace>Einzel.Wpf</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>einzel-shell</AssemblyName>

    <!--
      Lets the project restore and compile on Linux, where the Windows targeting
      packs are not installed by default. It does not make it runnable there and is
      not meant to: what CI checks on Linux is that nothing below the shell has
      acquired a dependency on it, and the shell compiling is a bonus.
    -->
    <EnableWindowsTargeting>true</EnableWindowsTargeting>

    <Description>
      The shell. Spec section 16: WPF on Windows, eleven required views. UI-1 is
      the constraint that shapes it - the shell owns layout, input, the interactive
      viewport and the update check, and owns no physics, no validation rules, no
      file format knowledge and no render output. Amendment 25 adds that every
      action here must be expressible as a CLI invocation and journalled as one, so
      a capability with no command spelling cannot be added to the window.
    </Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Einzel.Commands\Einzel.Commands.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
